Dear Angel,

Re 3. Interpreting interactions is indeed somewhat complicated.
It is easier if you see the fitted logit values, or the corresponding 
hit rates, broken down by the cells of your design:

R> with(DatosAngelo,
       tapply( fitted(ModeloAngel), list(grupo,tipo), mean ))
or
R> with(DatosAngelo,
       antilogit( tapply( fitted(ModeloAngel), list(grupo,tipo), mean )))

The antilogit function is defined as follows (lifted from 
http://www.hugoquene.nl/R/hqfunctions.r):

##  Define a substitute function if necessary:
if(!exists("antilogit", mode="function"))
	antilogit <- function(x)
	{
	# compute and return antilogit of x;
	# this returns a proportion p for which logit(p)==x;
		return( exp(x)/(1+exp(x)) )
	}

The interaction means that the proportion of (respuest=="1") responses 
(correct responses??) for the combination of grupoOSV and syntactic 
violation is higher than one would expect from the main effect of 
grupoOSV (intercept+grupoOSV = 1.8+0.3) and the main effect of tipo 
(intercept+typeFVsintact = 1.8-1.5). The combined effect is 
1.8+0.3-1.5+0.8 = 1.4 in logit units (or 0.80 as a proportion), whereas 
the expected value without interaction is 1.8+0.3-1.5 = 0.6 in logit 
units (or 0.65 as a proportion). Grupo OSV yields a higher response rate 
(relative to your reference), but ONLY for the syntactic violations. The 
syntactic violations yield a negative effect (relative to your 
reference), but this negative effect is less negative (significantly 
more positive, by 0.8 logit units) for the OSV grupo.

BTW, it may be better to include sentences as an additional random 
effect, so you may end up with two crossed random effects.

Hope this helps! Best wishes, Hugo Quené


