Hi Angel,

First, double-check how you have your data coded.  For example, there
are negative effects for both kinds of violations.  If your DV is
coded "1" for accurate responses (i.e., responding "correct" for
grammatical and "incorrect" for ungrammatical), then that means that
the violation conditions are less accurate (which could perhaps
indicate a bias for accepting sentences as okay).  But if your DV is
coded "1" for responding "correct" (i.e., which would be accurate for
grammatical items and inaccurate for ungrammatical items), then these
negative effects could simply indicate that subjects were sensitive to
the difference between grammatical and ungrammatical, being less
likely to call ungrammatical items "correct" that they are to respond
that way to grammatical items.  Either mode of coding is legitimate;
just make sure you've picked one that helps you make sense of your
results.

As to your question about how to go about getting all the pairwise
comparisons, I have been thinking (and worrying) about this some
myself, and I would be interested to hear if anyone else on the list
has specific suggestions.  You *can* do what you mention and shift the
reference levels around, which is a sure way to get estimates for each
pairwise comparison.  But in my mind this is starting to slip into the
realm of "multiple comparisons," and there is a worry that you could
be finding some spurious differences.  I have not seen any corrections
for mixed models analogous to Bonferroni corrections on ANOVAs.
There's Gelman's paper saying we shouldn't worry
(http://www.stat.columbia.edu/~gelman/research/published/multiple2f.pdf),
but that's more in the Bayesian context, and I'm not much of a
Bayesian (yet), so I can't say whether this extends to non-Bayesian
lmer() estimates.

But I think my suggestion would be to try to frame your research
question(s) in a succinct way that allows you to examine just a few
comparisons, which will also help you present your results to others.
Fortunately, at a glance it looks like many of your effects are large
enough that you shouldn't run into too many problems, whichever way
you turn the factors.  But sometimes you can get what seem like
ambiguous or contradictory inferences when you start rotating the
factors through different reference levels.  Like if you have one
factor A/B and another factor 1/2, you can find that A:1 is no
different from B:1, but A:2 is different from B:2, but the interaction
does not come out significant.  When dealing with binary factors, you
can do things like centering to simplify the inferencing a bit, but
that's not an option in a case like yours with many levels.

Sorry, not a solution; just a suggestion that as far as I know,
there's nothing wrong with changing reference levels, but you should
proceed with caution and not get too overzealous about every pairwise
comparison.  But I'd be happy if someone on the list knows better!

good luck,
-scott

